//# #2745: an ASSIGNMENT that rebinds a slot to an aggregate.
//
// Codex, P1 round 13 on #2761. Measured on `2d6e2b4`:
// `{ let mut p = idf((a, 1)); p = (a, 1); match p { (b, _) => b } }` printed a
// 3-digit pointer decimal.
//
// The absorb walk pushed an assignment's CLASSIFICATION, and a tuple literal
// classifies as "" -- a tuple is not itself a formal. So this assignment
// pushed nothing, `p` kept the shape its unclassifiable initializer gave it
// (none), and the destructuring blanked `b`. An assignment rebinds the slot, so
// it now masks and records exactly as a `let` does, through the same recorder.
//
// `idf` is a nested lambda, so the initializer is the one this walk declines --
// which is what leaves the ASSIGNMENT as the only thing that can say what `p`
// holds.
//
// ROUND 15 NOTE -- this fixture is no longer isolated. `idf` was chosen as an
// initializer this walk DECLINES, so the route named above was the only thing
// that could refuse the program. Round 15 taught the walk to classify a call to
// a local generic lambda, so the initializer alone now refuses it too (measured:
// `{ let mut y = idf(a); y }` is refused on its own). The file still guards
// against the program compiling; it no longer attributes the refusal to this
// route. Re-isolating it needs a T-typed expression the pass cannot classify,
// and the supply of those shrinks every round -- which is #2773's argument, and
// the reason this note is here rather than a hunt for a more obscure spelling.

struct Qt {
  w: Int
}

fn outer[T](x: T) -> String {
  let inner = [T](a: T) -> String {
    let idf = [U](u: U) -> U {
      u
    }
    "\{ { let mut p = idf((a, 1))
    p = (a, 1)
    match p {
      (b, _) => b
    } } }"
  }
  inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })
}

export fn _start() -> Int {
  String::length(outer(7))
}
